1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e5 Nfd7 6. Bxe7 Qxe7 7. f4 a6 8. Qg4 f6 9. Bd3 g6 10. h4 f5 11. Qg3 c5 12. dxc5 Nxc5 13. h5 Rg8 14. Be2 g5 15. h6 Nc6 16. Bh5+ Kf8 17. Be2 ({"} 17. Nf3 {is more urgent than ever" (The Field)}) 17... gxf4 18. Qxf4 Rxg2 19. Nh3 Rg8 20. a3 Nd7 21. Bh5 Ndxe5 22. O-O-O Ng6 23. Bxg6 Rxg6 24. Rhg1 Ra7 25. Rxg6 hxg6 26. Ng5 Kg8 27. Rh1 d4 28. h7+ Kh8 {(n.b. The Field gives a diagram with this position but with the caption 'Position after White's 27th move')} 29. Ne2 e5 30. Qh4 b6 31. Qh6 Qf6 32. Ng3 Qg7 33. Qh4 Rc7 34. Qh2 d3 {(score gives 'P to Q5')} 35. Qd2 dxc2 36. Kxc2 f4 37. N3e4 Nd4+ 38. Kb1 Bf5 39. a4 Qd7 40. Qb4 Qd8 41. Qxb6 Rc1+ ({"Pressed for time, Mr Wooldridge made this hasty move which loses.} 41... Rd7 {would have won the game." (The Field, 1 April 1911 - however, this is wrong as White has} 42. Nf7+ $1 {with mate in a further three moves.}) ({The correct way to meet 41 Qxb6 would have been} 41... Bxe4+ $1 {which does win for Black.}) 42. Rxc1 {"After 42 R takes R, the game was given in favour of White by Messrs Hoffer and Ward." (Source: The Field, 1 April 1911). However, according to the Westminster Gazette for 1 April 1911, the game continued for two further half-moves. Also, the comment in The Field is wrong anyway as Black could have replied to 42 Rxc1 with 42...Qxb6 and won. JS} (42. Ka2 $3 Be6+ $1 $15) ({The next issue of The Field (8 April 1911) included an answer to a reader's query: "V. R. G. (Alvechurch) - it should be 42. K takes R...} 42. Kxc1 {, and Black cannot save the game." But it's wrong since} Ne2+ $1 {still wins for Black.}) 42... Bxe4+ $4 (42... Qxb6 $19) 43. Ka2 {Adjudicated a win for White. Sources: Westminster Gazette, 1 April 1911; The Field, 1 April 1911 (with score and commentary errors as described above)} 1-0

